{"moveId":"science-and-faith-enrich","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"McGrath presents faith as enriching the significance of science rather than competing with scientific inquiry.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"religion versus science this is about my realization as I saw it that in effect this way of seeing things gave a new significance to my science that in effect it enriched my vision of what science was and what it was all about and as I sought science and Faith came together not to compete but rather to","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move rejects science-versus-religion competition, presenting faith as an interpretive framework enriching science and supporting McGrath’s central Christian-explanatory-worldview burden, with logical coherence 72, responsiveness 76, precision and clarity 74, and calibration and charity 81. Principal limitation: Personal enrichment shows usefulness, not truth, and offers no independent evidence that Christianity’s significance accurately describes reality, matching evidence warrant 55 and moderate relevance to burden 71. Live burden: With no direct reply identified, McGrath must explain what faith adds beyond subjective meaning, specify how that contribution is evaluated, and answer Shermer’s distinction between personally coherent experience and publicly justified belief. Locked score: The locked 70 credits a noncompetitive synthesis, though the inference from enriched scientific vision to God being more than imaginary remains unstated and evidentially incomplete."}}
{"moveId":"recurring-resurrection-myths","type":"fallacy","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"appeal-to-authority","label":"Appeal to authority","definition":"Relying on a person or institution's status as if it settles the argument, especially when the relevant evidence, expertise, or consensus has not been shown.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/appeal-to-authority/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Resurrection stories predate Christianity and recur across cultures through diffusion or independent creation of a limited set of mythic themes.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"Resurrection myth so Cyrus was the first deity that was resurrected in Egypt 2400 years before Jesus these are common myths that come up over and over just think of sort of a Joseph Campbell deconstruction of myths around the world and how common these themes come up either by diffusion or more likely through simultaneous creation because there's only so many stories in Hollywood for example and in in Mythic stories like this","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move gives a pattern beyond religious diversity, proposing diffusion and independent creation as human mechanisms for recurring resurrection narratives and advancing Shermer’s central comparative-religion-origin burden, with logical coherence 74, responsiveness 82, relevance to burden 80, and precision and clarity 70. Principal limitation: The historical example lacks corroboration and careful comparison, while resemblance proves neither equivalence nor derivation, matching evidence warrant 57 and calibration and charity 67 toward potentially distinctive Christian claims. Live burden: No direct reply exists, so Shermer must substantiate chronology, define similarities, compare alternative explanations, and show why recurrence makes independent divine reality less likely instead of merely showing cultures reuse broad narrative motifs. Locked score: The locked 72 rewards relevant, responsive, plausible explanation, but thin evidence and comparative controls limit its stronger causal inference."}}
