{"moveId":"ungrounded-values-reflect-society","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"McGrath characterizes meaning or value here as a hypothesization of societal prejudices and raises the question of what such things can be based on.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"for things like meaning or value then in effect it's simply the hypothesization of the the in effect the prejudices of a society so I'm really trying to get at this question of what we actually able to base things on yeah and I I fully","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move identifies a genuine grounding problem for Shermer's internal-validation distinction, asking whether socially generated meaning and value can escape inherited prejudice and thereby making substantial contact with McGrath's transcendent-value burden. Principal limitation: The formulation is imprecise and largely interrogative, treating social origin as presumptively prejudicial without showing why critical reflection, evidence, or shared interests cannot correct values, which depresses coherence, warrant, clarity, and charity. Live burden: McGrath must state the inference cleanly, demonstrate why naturalistic standards collapse into social preference, and explain how Christian grounding avoids parallel interpretive and historical distortions. Locked score: The 68 preserves credit for relevant pressure on secular normativity and reasonable responsiveness, but records that the claim is under-supported and ambiguously expressed rather than a developed argument for God or objective value."}}
{"moveId":"secular-values-risk-projection","type":"fallacy","label":"Special pleading","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"special-pleading","label":"Special pleading","definition":"Applying an exception or different standard to one side of the argument without giving a principled reason for the difference.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/special-pleading/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Generating values naturalistically does not stabilize them objectively, because people may define desired values as self-evident projections of innate preferences.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"the way I see it but the question of how you stabilize it will actually say this seems to be not simply me projecting my desires because the point I was trying to make is we very often simply take what we want to be true and create that in terms of values that you then Define to be self-evidently true and what what objective basis do we have for actually doing that that's not simply a rareification of our innate desires yes","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: McGrath separates causal genealogy from objective justification, answering Shermer’s evolutionary account that useful preferences can be projected as self-evident, preserving the six-dimension pattern of responsiveness and burden relevance, charity and coherence, adequate clarity, and comparatively weak evidence. Principal limitation: Projection’s possibility does not establish secular standards as unstable, while McGrath gives no evidence that transcendent grounding is available or less projective, leaving evidential warrant beneath reply contact. Live burden: McGrath must demonstrate why Shermer’s candidate standards cannot achieve intersubjective or objective force, then explain how Christianity identifies, accesses, and corrects moral truth without replicating human preference. Locked score: The 81 rewards a focused, charitable rebuttal exposing a missing normative bridge, with a bounded deduction because undermining naturalistic derivation does not itself prove divine grounding."}}
{"moveId":"meaning-available-without-religion","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Scientific understanding can be existentially meaningful, and people can obtain purpose, meaning, and love with or without religion.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"a scientific maybe you can't prove that scientifically an experiment but the the elegant writing about what science has revealed to us is deeply existentially meaningful and so from that we can derive purpose all the things that that you're concerned we're all concerned about purpose meaning love these things we can we can get all that with without religion you can get it with religion without religion so it's not necessary to have that","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: Shermer counters faith’s existential advantage by distinguishing access to meaning from religious truth, affirming scientific understanding, love, and purpose can structure nonreligious lives, preserving the six-dimension pattern of exceptional charity and responsiveness, burden relevance, coherence, clarity, and comparatively weak evidence. Principal limitation: The experiential assertion establishes availability rather than philosophical adequacy and offers limited evidence that secular meaning answers McGrath’s objective-grounding concern, leaving evidential warrant below coherence and reply contact. Live burden: Shermer must clarify whether the standard is psychological availability, durable justification, or objective value, then show his naturalistic account satisfies it rather than reporting comparable fulfillment. Locked score: The 80 credits a relevant, carefully calibrated rebuttal to religious exclusivity, while withholding stronger recognition because lived meaningfulness does not resolve the grounding dispute."}}
{"moveId":"science-does-not-answer-why","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Science explains how humans came to be but does not answer the important existential question of why humans are here.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"one of the things that really interests me is this I mean I would say science is very very good telling us how we came to be here but it doesn't answer the question why we're here now you might say that in fact you know that that is a spurious question but again it's interesting and important for us so I mean surely we have to keep this this language of why open even though it","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move charitably separates science’s causal account of how humanity arose from the existential question of why human life matters, directly answering Shermer’s accounts of scientific meaning and evolved values. Principal limitation: “Why” remains ambiguous among purpose, cause, and desired significance, while showing that science alone does not answer it establishes neither Christianity’s answer nor that an external answer exists, leaving evidence warrant weaker than coherence, responsiveness, relevance, clarity, and charity. Live burden: McGrath must specify the explanatory target, defend its legitimacy, and show how theism provides a testable or otherwise warranted answer instead of occupying territory outside scientific method. Locked score: The 77 rewards the stronger dimensions and conceptual separation, but remains limited because identifying science’s boundary does not positively support God’s reality."}}
{"moveId":"science-does-not-answer-why","type":"fallacy","label":"Equivocation","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"equivocation","label":"Equivocation","definition":"Shifting the meaning of a key word or phrase during the argument, making the reasoning seem stronger than it is.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/equivocation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Science explains how humans came to be but does not answer the important existential question of why humans are here.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"one of the things that really interests me is this I mean I would say science is very very good telling us how we came to be here but it doesn't answer the question why we're here now you might say that in fact you know that that is a spurious question but again it's interesting and important for us so I mean surely we have to keep this this language of why open even though it","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move charitably separates science’s causal account of how humanity arose from the existential question of why human life matters, directly answering Shermer’s accounts of scientific meaning and evolved values. Principal limitation: “Why” remains ambiguous among purpose, cause, and desired significance, while showing that science alone does not answer it establishes neither Christianity’s answer nor that an external answer exists, leaving evidence warrant weaker than coherence, responsiveness, relevance, clarity, and charity. Live burden: McGrath must specify the explanatory target, defend its legitimacy, and show how theism provides a testable or otherwise warranted answer instead of occupying territory outside scientific method. Locked score: The 77 rewards the stronger dimensions and conceptual separation, but remains limited because identifying science’s boundary does not positively support God’s reality."}}
{"moveId":"selective-credit-defies-falsification","type":"bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"confirmation-bias","label":"Confirmation bias","definition":"Seeking, interpreting, or remembering evidence in ways that favor an existing view while discounting contrary information.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Crediting God for good outcomes but never blaming God for bad ones makes the belief system compatible with every result and therefore unfalsifiable.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"survived that's just random God's not present at all say well yes he was present you know so here's the problem with you know prayer and all this stuff you know when good things happen God gets the credit and when bad things happen who gets the blame not God uh so no matter what happens it fulfills the the belief system so how could we ever falsify it","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move exposes the asymmetry in Shermer's excerpt by showing that crediting God for favorable outcomes while never blaming God for unfavorable ones makes every result compatible with belief and directly answers McGrath's practical-relief reply. Principal limitation: The critique assumes believers apply selective accounting uniformly and insufficiently considers theological criteria distinguishing providence, permission, and human agency, so its generalization needs closer response-specific demonstration. Live burden: Shermer must establish that McGrath's actual framework uses this asymmetry, identify an observation that would count against intervention, and show that alternative theological explanations are independently unprincipled rather than merely complex. Locked score: The 81 reflects coherence 86, warrant 73, responsiveness 82, burden relevance 88, precision 85, and charity 71, preserving an exceptionally clear, relevant diagnostic with meaningful evidential support."}}
{"moveId":"faith-motivates-relief","type":"fallacy","label":"Red herring","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"red-herring","label":"Red herring","definition":"Diverting attention from the issue under dispute to a related but less relevant point, leaving the original challenge insufficiently answered.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Christianity identifies suffering as wrong and motivates believers to relieve it and improve the world within their limited capacities.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"that's absolutely clear one of the points I'd want to make though is that the Christian vision of reality is to say suffering is wrong and therefore they they sort of the feeling of so many Christians they want to get involved in relief they want to get involved in the medical profession is in effect a desire to try and make this world a better place through their own limited capacity because this is what God would want them to do","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: McGrath's excerpt gives suffering practical consequence by saying Christianity calls it wrong and motivates limited believers toward relief and medicine, coherently presenting faith's action-guiding ethical role with notable clarity and charitable restraint. Principal limitation: Motivation to alleviate suffering neither answers Shermer's inference about omnipotent divine intervention nor supplies evidence that religious motivation uniquely or reliably produces relief, leaving responsiveness particularly weak and warrant only moderate. Live burden: McGrath must connect human action to suffering's compatibility with God's power and compassion, distinguish practical fruit from truth, and explain why divine action permits the unequal harms under discussion. Locked score: The 72 reflects coherence 78, warrant 64, responsiveness 63, burden relevance 71, precision 81, and charity 86, crediting meaningful moral relevance while recognizing substantial noncontact with the evidential problem."}}
{"moveId":"jesus-claimed-historically-reliable","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"McGrath believes the Jesus story is historically reliable and deeply significant while explicitly conceding that he cannot prove it.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"um the story of Jesus Christ is something that I believe to be historically reliable well I know I can't prove that but uh there's something very significant about this they're in effect is saying this is saying something very deep about what","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The explicit concession that proof is unavailable shows commendable calibration and states McGrath's historical commitment clearly, preserving a modest response to Shermer's demand rather than pretending certainty the move cannot establish. Principal limitation: Historical reliability and deep significance are asserted without supporting evidence, and conceding nonproof does not answer either proportional-evidence scrutiny or the insulation concern, producing extremely weak warrant, responsiveness, and coherence relative to charity. Live burden: McGrath must present reasons for historical reliability, compare resurrection with alternative explanations, and show why significance supports truth rather than merely personal commitment. Locked score: The 62 records limited clarity and exceptional modesty but an evidentially underdeveloped reply, because the central historical premise receives no defense and therefore cannot carry the Christian explanatory-worldview burden against Shermer's specific objections."}}
{"moveId":"jesus-claimed-historically-reliable","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"McGrath believes the Jesus story is historically reliable and deeply significant while explicitly conceding that he cannot prove it.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"um the story of Jesus Christ is something that I believe to be historically reliable well I know I can't prove that but uh there's something very significant about this they're in effect is saying this is saying something very deep about what","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The explicit concession that proof is unavailable shows commendable calibration and states McGrath's historical commitment clearly, preserving a modest response to Shermer's demand rather than pretending certainty the move cannot establish. Principal limitation: Historical reliability and deep significance are asserted without supporting evidence, and conceding nonproof does not answer either proportional-evidence scrutiny or the insulation concern, producing extremely weak warrant, responsiveness, and coherence relative to charity. Live burden: McGrath must present reasons for historical reliability, compare resurrection with alternative explanations, and show why significance supports truth rather than merely personal commitment. Locked score: The 62 records limited clarity and exceptional modesty but an evidentially underdeveloped reply, because the central historical premise receives no defense and therefore cannot carry the Christian explanatory-worldview burden against Shermer's specific objections."}}
{"moveId":"old-resurrection-story-insulated","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"The resurrection story's antiquity allows it to be insulated by theology, but does not answer how anyone knows it occurred.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"but that I just kind of feel like the Christian Resurrection story is old enough you know kind of lost in the midst of time that you can kind of bury it behind theological arguments but at some point how do you know","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move presses the decisive epistemic question against McGrath's beyond-proof framework, arguing that antiquity and theological elaboration can protect a claim without supplying knowledge, thereby maintaining strong response contact and burden relevance. Principal limitation: The suggestion of insulation is asserted without examining the actual historical arguments, and age alone neither discredits testimony nor demonstrates deliberate evasion, which explains the weak evidence warrant and reduced charitable calibration. Live burden: Shermer must identify the insulating mechanisms, evaluate the available sources, and distinguish legitimate historical inference from theological protection before concluding that the resurrection claim lacks adequate support. Locked score: The 72 credits a clear, relevant skeptical challenge with respectable coherence and responsiveness, while reflecting that its characterization of the evidence remains generalized and rhetorically stronger than the move's demonstrated case."}}
{"moveId":"meaning-without-jesus","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"Jewish and Muslim believers can have equally deep, thoughtful, and purposeful worldviews without accepting Jesus as savior or resurrected.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"I would point out that of course Jews and and Muslims who presumably believe in the same God but not accepting Jesus as a savior or who was resurrected or any of that and it seems to me uh Jews and Muslims have the same equally deep thoughtful existentially purposeful a worldview","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move uses nearby theistic counterexamples to separate existential depth from specifically Christian truth, directly answering McGrath's historical claim while remaining notably charitable toward Jewish, Muslim, and Christian sources of meaning. Principal limitation: Comparable purpose shows that Christianity is not uniquely necessary for meaningful life, but it does not adjudicate the competing traditions' truth or directly refute resurrection, keeping warrant below coherence, responsiveness, clarity, and calibration. Live burden: Shermer must demonstrate why plural existential success favors human construction rather than one true tradition alongside meaningful error, and compare evidence rather than fulfillment alone. Locked score: The 79 recognizes a precise, relevant, and well-calibrated comparative rebuttal with strong response contact, while remaining just below the 80s because it undermines an exclusivity inference more securely than the historical claim itself."}}
{"moveId":"sentient-flourishing-moral-standard","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"The survival and flourishing of sentient individuals provides a moral starting point, with actions that reduce suffering counted as good.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"survival and flourishing of sentient beings so of individual sentient beings so uh and I use that instead of people because you know we now know that a lot of animals are sentient and feel and suffer and as Jeremy Bentham said it's not can they talk or reason but can they suffer so the suffering of sentient beings is is sort of the starting point so things that lower suffering are good","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The move supplies a concrete non-theological standard centered on sentient survival, flourishing, and suffering, directly answering demands for a framework beyond preference and concerns that secular values are projected. Principal limitation: Treating reduced suffering as good still needs a normative bridge, while conflicts among survival, flourishing, individuals, and sentient species remain unresolved, so responsiveness, relevance, clarity, and charity exceed evidence warrant and coherence. Live burden: Shermer must directly justify sentience's moral authority, explain measurement and tradeoffs, and show how the standard resolves clashes between flourishing and suffering across individuals or species. Locked score: The locked 80 rewards a clear, responsive, substantially grounded moral proposal with excellent burden contact while deducting for a plausible but incompletely defended and operationalized starting point."}}
