{"moveId":"faith-motivates-good-closing","type":"bias","label":"Subjective validation","definition":{"type":"bias","slug":"subjective-validation","label":"Subjective validation","definition":"Treating a claim as especially accurate or meaningful because it feels personally resonant or confirms one's private experience.","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/subjective-validation/"},"moveEvidence":{"lockedProposition":"McGrath finds in faith a worldview and motivation for action, which is why he concludes that God remains an important and valid idea.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"motivating factor and for me I find that in faith which actually generates this way of seeing the world and this desire to do something about it so for me that that is why I think God remains an important idea and a valid idea and","assessmentRationale":"Strongest feature: The closing move connects worldview to motivation and action, explaining why faith remains existentially important for McGrath and giving his case a practical motion-level synthesis with good charity, clarity, and responsiveness. Principal limitation: A belief's motivational usefulness does not establish its validity, and the move provides no evidence that faith uniquely generates beneficial action or accurately represents God, which leaves evidence warrant markedly below its strongest dimensions. Live burden: McGrath must defend the inference from practical motivation to truth, compare religious and nonreligious sources of action, and connect his personal experience to reality beyond imagination rather than subjective importance. Locked score: The 71 recognizes a relevant, intelligible conclusion with moderate coherence and calibration, while reflecting that its central transition from valuable motivation to valid idea remains evidentially unsupported."}}

